S += Tb([['Outcome', 'Pairs', 'Moved', 'Twin', 'Difference [95% CI]', 'p', 'E-value']] +
        [['Share of league minutes', mA['pairs'], f"{100 * mA['treated_mean_change']:+.1f} pts", f"{100 * mA['twin_mean_change']:+.1f} pts", f"{100 * mA['att']:+.1f} pts [{100 * mA['ci95'][0]:+.1f} to {100 * mA['ci95'][1]:+.1f}]", fmt_p(mA['p_sign_flip']), f2(mA['e_value'])],
         ['Style-neutral output index (0-100)', oA['pairs'], f"{oA['treated_mean_change']:+.1f}", f"{oA['twin_mean_change']:+.1f}", f"{zf(oA['att'])} [{oA['ci95'][0]:+.1f} to {oA['ci95'][1]:+.1f}]", fmt_p(oA['p_sign_flip']), f2(oA['e_value'])]] +
        [[f"Minutes, causal forest, age {g}", CFm[g]['treated'], '', '', f"{100 * CFm[g]['cate']:+.1f} pts [{100 * CFm[g]['ci95'][0]:+.1f} to {100 * CFm[g]['ci95'][1]:+.1f}]", fmt_p(CFm[g]['p']), ''] for g in ('21 or under', '22', '23-24')],
        [58, 13, 22, 22, 40, 12, 13], f"Effect of an elite move. Top rows: matched pairs with bootstrap intervals. Bottom rows: a causal forest (CausalForestDML) on all {n_(Bq['feeders'])} feeders; the pairs column counts the treated players in each group.")
S += F('figs/p4b_sliding_doors.png', 'Left: covariate balance before and after matching. Centre: the causal forest\'s estimate by age and position. Right: the change in each outcome for movers and their twins.', 1.0)
S += [P(f"Both groups lose minutes over two years, by {abs(100 * mA['treated_mean_change']):.0f} and {abs(100 * mA['twin_mean_change']):.0f} points. That is regression to the mean: everyone in the sample was chosen for having played a lot. The difference between them is {100 * mA['att']:+.1f} points with an interval from {100 * mA['ci95'][0]:+.1f} to {100 * mA['ci95'][1]:+.1f}, and for output {zf(oA['att'])} index points. The causal forest finds no age or position where the sign changes."),
      P(f"So the familiar warning that a big move costs a young player his minutes is not supported here, and neither is the hope that it lifts his output. The estimate rules out a large effect either way, not a small one. Output is only observed for the {pc(Bq['output_observed_pairs_share'])} of pairs where both players kept playing, and clubs choose whom to buy on things we cannot see; with E-values this close to 1, a weak hidden factor could move the estimate.")]
S += [P("6C. Can a player game the scout?", H2),
      P(f"Four of the sixteen metrics are volume a player controls: {', '.join(NM[c] for c in Cq['cheap'])}. We took every player ranked 11 to 20 in {Cq['queries']} unseen replacement lists and let a greedy search raise those four, never beyond the 95th percentile for his position, re-scoring him through the whole pipeline each time."),
      P(f"{pc(Cq['gameability'], 1)} of them reach the top 10. The padding needed is small: typically {Cq['median_added_per90'][lever]:.2f} more {NM[lever]} per 90, about {Cq['median_added_in_yoy_sd'][lever]:.2f} of the amount that metric moves from one season to the next for the same player anyway. Nobody would notice. Further down the list it stops working: {pc(Cq['by_rank_band']['21-30'])} of players ranked 21 to 30 can do it, {pc(Cq['by_rank_band']['31-40'])} of those ranked 31 to 40 and {pc(Cq['by_rank_band']['41-50'], 1)} beyond. Padding promotes a near-miss; it does not manufacture a candidate."),
      P(f"We then hardened the model so that it never sees the four cheap metrics. Recall@10 on {HH['test_queries']} unseen events is {pc(HH['recall10_after'])} against {pc(HH['recall10_before'])}, so the cheap metrics were carrying little. But an attacker adapts. We gave a genetic-algorithm attacker a budget and let it raise any metric, at a cost of 1 per step for a cheap metric and {Cq['adaptive_attack']['cost_of_costly_step']} for a costly one.")]
S += Tb([['Attack budget'] + [str(b_) for b_ in BUDS], ['Engine: near-miss players who reach the top 10'] + [pc(curve[('engine', b_)]) for b_ in BUDS], ['Hardened model'] + [pc(curve[('hardened', b_)]) for b_ in BUDS]], [70] + [100 / len(BUDS)] * len(BUDS), f"Adaptive attack by a genetic algorithm on {Cq['adaptive_attack']['players_per_model']} near-miss players per model. One step is 5% of the position's range in a metric.")
S += F('figs/p4b_redteam.png', 'Left: share of near-miss players the adaptive attacker lifts into the top 10, by budget. Right: how far down the list padding cheap statistics still works.', 1.0)
S += [P(f"Hardening buys protection only against small efforts. At budgets of {BUDS[0]} and {BUDS[1]} the hardened model gives nothing away ({pc(curve[('hardened', BUDS[0])])} and {pc(curve[('hardened', BUDS[1])])}) where the engine gives {pc(curve[('engine', BUDS[0])])} and {pc(curve[('engine', BUDS[1])])}. Once the budget pays for a single costly step the two are alike, and at {BUDS[-1]} the attacker succeeds against the hardened model {pc(curve[('hardened', BUDS[-1])])} of the time. A player who really improves an outcome metric should rise, of course; the model cannot tell a better player from a better-looking one, and neither can a scout reading the same table. The cheap and costly labels are our judgement, and the attack changes one season of numbers while holding market value fixed.")]
S += [P("6D. Rebuilding several positions on one budget", H2),
      P(f"A club that loses three starters has one budget for all three. Each open position has its sixteen best-ranked candidates; the task is to take one per position, maximise the summed fit, stay inside what the departed starters were worth, and never pick a player twice. Our genetic algorithm encodes each position in four bits and runs 60 squads for 40 generations. Integer programming gives the true optimum."),
      P(f"Over {Dq['instances']} real club-summers with two to six starters to replace, the genetic algorithm found the exact optimum in {pc(Dq['share_reaching_optimum'])} and was never worse than {pc(Dq['min_ratio'], 1)} of it. The best squad usually appears by generation {Dq['median_generation_of_best']:.0f}. For a problem this size the integer program is faster ({1000 * Dq['mean_ilp_seconds']:.0f} ms against {1000 * Dq['mean_ga_seconds']:.0f} ms), and scoring each generation as a Spark job costs {Dq['mean_ga_spark_seconds']:.1f} seconds of overhead for an identical answer. The genetic algorithm earns its place when the objective stops being linear, as it does for the attacker above. The dashboard runs both and shows them side by side.")]
S += F('figs/p4b_rebuild.png', 'The genetic algorithm\'s squad as a share of the integer-programming optimum, one bar per club-summer.', 0.6)

# ---------- 7 ----------
S += section('7', 'Predictions, explained', 'Dashboard, SHAP, radar')
S += [P("The dashboard is a Streamlit app that reads the Parquet lake. It opens on a club's starters drawn on a pitch. Choosing one opens his shadow list as cards: rank with its range across the ten models, value beside the starter's, and the reasons for the rank in plain words, taken from the three largest SHAP contributions. A compare view sets the candidate's radar against the starter's. A toggle switches between the live season and any backtest season, with a badge saying which is on screen."),
      P(f"The three backtest cases below were picked by rule: the unseen events where the real signing ranked best, ties broken by the larger fee. The fourth is the unseen event where the real signing ranked worst. Across all unseen events the scores that move a rank most are {', '.join(k.replace('_', ' ').replace('log value', 'market value').replace('age num', 'age') for k in list(p6['shap_global']['mean_abs_shap'])[::-1][:3])}.")]
CASES = {c['tag']: c for c in p6['cases']}
def fee_txt(c): return 'a fee Transfermarkt does not record' if c['fee'] is None else ('no fee' if c['fee'] == 0 else f"EUR {c['fee'] / 1e6:.0f}m")
def pctl(c, f): return c['signed_feature_percentile_in_pool'][f]
WRITTEN = {
 'hit_ManchesterCity_2024_Aït-Nouri': lambda c: f"<b>Manchester City, summer 2025.</b> Rico Lewis had started regularly in 2024-25 and then all but dropped out of the side. City bought Rayan Aït-Nouri from Wolves for {fee_txt(c)}. He was first of {c['pool']} on our list. The engine did not get there through style: his translated similarity to Lewis was only middling (better than {100 * pctl(c, 'sim_translated'):.0f}% of the pool). It got there because he was valued almost exactly as Lewis was (EUR {c['signed_value'] / 1e6:.0f}m against EUR {c['starter_value'] / 1e6:.0f}m) and had the hazard profile of a player about to be bought. This is the engine's commonest route to a right answer, and it is closer to reading the market than to reading the player.",
 'hit_Rennes_2022_Fée': lambda c: f"<b>Rennes, summer 2023.</b> Flavien Tait, a midfield regular the season before, left the picture. Rennes paid {fee_txt(c)} to bring Enzo Le Fée from Lorient, and he was first of {c['pool']}. Unlike the City case this one is about the football. Le Fée's numbers translated into Rennes's style sat closer to Tait's than {100 * pctl(c, 'sim_translated'):.0f}% of the pool, his recent seasons had developed the same way (better than {100 * pctl(c, 'traj_sim'):.0f}%), and his output for the position was near the top. A Breton club buying a Breton club's best midfielder is also the kind of same-league move the live engine has learned to expect.",
 'hit_RealSociedad_2021_Kubo': lambda c: f"<b>Real Sociedad, summer 2022.</b> Adnan Januzaj's contract ran out and he left. The club signed Takefusa Kubo from Real Madrid for {fee_txt(c)}; he had spent the previous season on loan at Mallorca, which is where our data saw him. First of {c['pool']}. His translated profile was closer to Januzaj's than {100 * pctl(c, 'sim_translated'):.0f}% of the pool: a left-footed winger who carries the ball and creates more than he scores, replacing another. Here the radar and the ranking agree, and the profile did the work."}
MISS = lambda c: f"<b>The miss: {esc(c['club'])}, summer {c['season'] + 1}.</b> {esc(c['starter'])} left and the club signed {esc(c['signed'])} from {esc(c['from'])} for {fee_txt(c)}. We ranked him {c['rank']} of {c['pool']}. On the page he looked nothing like the man he replaced, and he was valued at EUR {c['signed_value'] / 1e6:.0f}m against EUR {c['starter_value'] / 1e6:.0f}m. What made the deal attractive was a contract running out, which let a club take an experienced player without a fee. We have no contract dates for past seasons, so the engine was blind to the one fact that mattered."
GEN = lambda c: f"<b>{esc(c['club'])}, summer {c['season'] + 1}.</b> {esc(c['starter'])} ({c['position']}) was replaced by {esc(c['signed'])} from {esc(c['from'])} for {fee_txt(c)}. We ranked him {c['rank']} of {c['pool']}."
first = True
for c in [c for c in p6['cases'] if c['kind'] == 'hit']:
    S += [P(WRITTEN.get(c['tag'], GEN)(c))]
    if first: S += F('', f"{esc(c['club'])} in the dashboard's backtest mode: the squad on the pitch, with the starters who left filled in.", 0.84, path=crop(f"screens/{c['tag']}_squad.png", 0.0, 1.0)); first = False
    S += F('', f"{esc(c['signed'])} against {esc(c['starter'])}: radar, the reasons in words, values and value history.", 0.7, path=crop(f"screens/{c['tag']}_compare.png", 0.1, 1.0))
for c in [c for c in p6['cases'] if c['kind'] == 'miss']:
    S += [P(MISS(c))] + F('', f"What the list said instead for {esc(c['starter'])}'s position.", 0.74, path=crop(f"screens/{c['tag']}_cards.png", 0, 0.32))

# ---------- 8 ----------
S += section('8', 'Limitations and conclusion', 'What to trust')
S += bullets([f"<b>Coverage.</b> {pc(1 - p2['in_pool_share'])} of elite replacement signings came from outside our candidate pool, most from leagues we have no data for. Every test here is about moves inside the five leagues.",
              f"<b>The summer-2026 test is small.</b> {S26['signings']} early deals from one window. It agrees with the backtest; it does not prove it.",
              f"<b>Elite replacements are few.</b> {nq} unseen events. On that task no method can be separated from market value.",
              "<b>Sources differ.</b> La Liga 2024-25 is in neither Understat dump, so for that one league-season the live engine uses FBref's equivalents and lacks two metrics. The Transfermarkt dump has gaps in its transfer records, which we fill from playing minutes, and no loan flag.",
              "<b>Market value is a crowd's opinion.</b> We use it strictly before each window, but it still carries what the crowd expected.",
              "<b>Scale.</b> Everything runs in Spark's local mode on one machine. On data this size Spark is slower than plain Python for several tasks, and we say where."])
S += [Spacer(1, 3), P(f"Asking whom a club will sign, instead of who will move up, gave us a test with a right answer. On it the live engine names the signing in its top 10 about {pc(BT[LE]['recall@10'])} of the time, against {pc(BT[LVF]['recall@10'])} for the best simple rule, and it kept that level on a summer it had never seen. Most of that skill comes from the structure of the market. Player statistics matter where the market is thin: below the median fee, where value alone names almost nobody."),
      P(f"The same data answered three questions we had not planned to ask. The market leaves money on the table for players who are cheap for their output. An elite move does little to a young player's path on average. And a ranking built on per-90 numbers can be nudged by anyone who knows which numbers are cheap. Style translation, which we expected to be the centre of the project, turned out not to help, and we have left it in the report where it failed.")]

